# Rule: SwiftUI Layout System

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/maintaining-the-adaptable-sizes-of-built-in-views
https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/scaling-views-to-complement-text
https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/organizing-and-aligning-content-with-stacks
https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/adjusting-the-space-between-views
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/layout-fundamentals
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/layout-adjustments
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/custom-layout

Scope

Applies to the structural layout of SwiftUI views:

- built-in layout containers
- sizing behavior
- alignment and spacing
- padding and frame adjustments
- adaptive text-related scaling
- escalation to custom layout

This rule governs how views are measured, arranged, adjusted, and adapted
across window sizes, content sizes, and dynamic type changes.

Intent

Build SwiftUI layouts that are:

- adaptive
- stable
- text-aware
- container-driven
- readable
- maintainable

Prefer built-in layout behavior first.
Use layout adjustments to refine behavior.
Escalate to custom layout only when built-in containers cannot express the requirement clearly.

Core Principle

SwiftUI layout is container-driven.

A parent container arranges child views according to layout rules.
Built-in containers should be the default tool.
Adjustment modifiers should refine layout rather than fight it.
Custom layout should be used only when the layout requirement is real and reusable.

The layout architecture must prioritize:

1. adaptability
2. semantic container choice
3. text-respecting sizing
4. explicit alignment and spacing decisions
5. minimal layout hacks

Architectural Translation

Translate Apple’s layout model into these project rules:

- Start with built-in layout containers.
- Let content determine size where possible.
- Let text remain readable and drive minimum useful size.
- Prefer alignment and spacing APIs over offset hacks.
- Use frames to constrain or negotiate layout intentionally, not blindly.
- Prefer adaptive switching between layouts over forcing one layout to fit all cases.
- Introduce custom layout only for real, repeated, domain-specific layout needs.

---

## LAYOUT STRATEGY ORDER

Always design layout in this order:

1. Choose the correct built-in container.
2. Let intrinsic content and default behavior work first.
3. Adjust spacing, alignment, padding, and frame constraints.
4. Add adaptation for text size, window size, or content density.
5. Introduce custom layout only if the above is insufficient.

Rules

- Never start with `GeometryReader` for ordinary screen layout.
- Never start with fixed sizes unless the element is truly fixed-size.
- Never use a custom layout when a stack/grid/frame solution is already clear.
- Never use position/offset as a primary layout system.

---

## BUILT-IN CONTAINERS FIRST

Use built-in layout containers as the default architecture.

Preferred built-in tools:

- `HStack`
- `VStack`
- `ZStack`
- `Grid`
- `Spacer`
- `Group`
- `ViewThatFits` where adaptation is needed

Rules

- Use stacks for one-dimensional relationships.
- Use grids for two-dimensional alignment problems.
- Use `ZStack` only for real layered composition.
- Use `Spacer` to distribute flexible empty space.
- Use `Group` for logical grouping, not layout.
- Prefer composing multiple built-in containers before designing a custom layout.

Good

VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 12) {
HeaderView()
SummaryCard()
ActionRow()
}
Good
Grid(alignment: .leading) {
GridRow {
Text("Client")
Text(client.name)
}
GridRow {
Text("Status")
StatusBadge(status: invoice.status)
}
}
Bad
ZStack {
VStack {
HStack { ... }
}
}
.offset(x: 10, y: -6)
This uses stacking and offsets to solve what should be a normal alignment problem.
LET CONTENT SIZE NATURALLY
SwiftUI layouts work best when content is allowed to express its natural size.
Rules

Avoid hard-coded widths and heights unless the design genuinely requires them.
Prefer minimum viable constraints over rigid dimensions.
Let text, controls, and images negotiate size with their container.
Treat fixed frames as exceptions, not defaults.
Do not make controls artificially small just to preserve a visual arrangement.
Good
Text(invoice.title)
.lineLimit(2)
Better constrained example
Text(invoice.title)
.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)
Bad
Text(invoice.title)
.frame(width: 120, height: 20)
This often truncates text and breaks adaptability.
TEXT DRIVES LAYOUT
Layout must complement text, not compete with it.
Rules

Prefer layouts that expand or reflow when text grows.
Support Dynamic Type and larger content sizes by default.
Avoid designing rows/cards that only work at one text size.
Prefer adaptive container changes over aggressive compression.
Prioritize readability over preserving a specific horizontal arrangement.
Good
ViewThatFits {
HStack {
Label("Due Date", systemImage: "calendar")
Text(invoice.dueDate.formatted(date: .abbreviated, time: .omitted))
}

    VStack(alignment: .leading) {
        Label("Due Date", systemImage: "calendar")
        Text(invoice.dueDate.formatted(date: .abbreviated, time: .omitted))
    }

}
Bad
HStack(spacing: 4) {
Text("Due Date")
Text(invoice.dueDateString)
}
.frame(width: 160)
This forces a single arrangement even when content no longer fits.
ALIGNMENT IS STRUCTURAL
Alignment should be chosen deliberately at the container level.
Rules

Use container alignment settings before introducing manual positional tweaks.
Align related text and controls to shared structural edges.
Prefer explicit alignment over relying on incidental default placement.
Keep alignment consistent across sibling sections of the same semantic kind.
Good
VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8) {
Text("Client")
Text(client.name)
Text(client.email)
}
Bad
VStack {
Text("Client")
.frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)
Text(client.name)
.padding(.leading, 3)
Text(client.email)
.padding(.leading, 11)
}
This replaces structural alignment with manual nudging.
SPACING MUST BE EXPLICIT AND SYSTEMATIC
Spacing is part of architecture, not decoration.
Rules

Use container spacing values intentionally.
Standardize spacing scales across the project.
Differentiate between:
internal spacing inside a component
spacing between sibling components
section spacing
screen-edge padding
Avoid stacking multiple unrelated padding calls to simulate spacing.
Preferred spacing model
4-8 points for tightly related inline elements
8-12 points for internal control/content grouping
12-20 points for section-level grouping
larger values only when creating clear visual separation
Good
VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 16) {
ClientHeader()
InvoiceTotalsSection()
PaymentStatusSection()
}
.padding(20)
Bad
VStack {
ClientHeader()
.padding(.bottom, 7)
InvoiceTotalsSection()
.padding(.bottom, 13)
PaymentStatusSection()
}
.padding(.top, 11)
.padding(.leading, 19)
.padding(.trailing, 18)
.padding(.bottom, 21)
This produces arbitrary spacing that is hard to maintain.
PADDING VS FRAME VS SPACER
Use each tool for its actual purpose.
Use:

padding to add interior/exterior breathing room around a view
frame to participate in sizing/alignment negotiation
Spacer to consume flexible remaining space
Rules
Do not use padding to fake alignment.
Do not use Spacer where fixed spacing is intended.
Do not use frame(maxWidth: .infinity) reflexively on every child.
Apply frame only when you want to influence proposal/placement behavior.
Good
HStack {
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
Text(client.name)
Text(client.email)
}
Spacer()
StatusBadge(status: client.status)
}
Bad
HStack {
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
Text(client.name)
Text(client.email)
}
Text(" ")
Text(" ")
Text(" ")
StatusBadge(status: client.status)
}
Bad
Text(client.name)
.padding(.leading, 40)
when the real need is parent-level alignment or spacer-driven distribution.
ADAPTIVE LAYOUT IS PREFERRED OVER COMPRESSION
When content, text, or window size changes, prefer switching layout strategies
instead of crushing content into the same arrangement.
Rules

Prefer alternate container arrangements over unreadable compression.
Use ViewThatFits or conditional composition when layout must change at narrower sizes.
Allow vertical expansion when horizontal packing becomes fragile.
Design for macOS window resizing explicitly.
Good
ViewThatFits(in: .horizontal) {
HStack(alignment: .top, spacing: 12) {
SummaryPane()
DetailPane()
}

    VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 16) {
        SummaryPane()
        DetailPane()
    }

}
Bad
HStack(spacing: 2) {
SummaryPane()
.frame(width: 140)
DetailPane()
.frame(width: 140)
}
This preserves one arrangement at the cost of flexibility.
CUSTOM LAYOUT ESCALATION
Use custom layout only when the layout requirement is:
real
reusable
not expressible cleanly with built-in containers
central to the feature’s behavior or design
Typical valid reasons:
equalizing child sizes based on measured ideal sizes
specialized inspector-style arrangements
domain-specific grid/board/canvas arrangements
animated transitions between layout types
placement logic that depends on subview measurement
Rules
Do not create a custom layout just to avoid learning stack/grid behavior.
Do not use custom layout for one-off cosmetic tweaks.
Prefer a local custom layout type scoped to the feature unless reuse is proven.
Measure subviews through layout APIs, not through hacky feedback loops.
Good use case
EqualWidthButtonRow {
Button("Create") { }
Button("Duplicate Invoice") { }
Button("Archive") { }
}
Bad use case
CustomRowLayout {
Text("Title")
Text("Value")
}
when a simple HStack or Grid already solves the problem.
DO NOT USE GEOMETRYREADER AS YOUR DEFAULT LAYOUT ENGINE
GeometryReader is for cases where container geometry is genuinely needed.
It is not the default answer for ordinary view arrangement.
Rules

Do not introduce GeometryReader just to align ordinary rows, columns, or cards.
Do not build parent-child measurement feedback loops for basic layout.
Prefer built-in containers, layout modifiers, or custom Layout conformance.
Use GeometryReader only when the child genuinely needs container geometry information.
Bad
GeometryReader { proxy in
HStack {
Text(title)
.frame(width: proxy.size.width _ 0.4)
Text(value)
.frame(width: proxy.size.width _ 0.6)
}
}
Better
Grid {
GridRow {
Text(title)
Text(value)
}
}
MACOS-SPECIFIC EXPECTATIONS
For macOS SwiftUI apps:
layouts must tolerate window resizing
sidebars, inspectors, and detail panes must adapt to changing width
tables/forms/toolbars must not rely on phone-sized assumptions
content should remain readable at large and small window sizes
inspector/detail layouts should degrade gracefully rather than clipping
Rules:
Design every major feature for narrow, medium, and wide window states.
Do not assume a full-width desktop window.
Avoid hard-coded widths for major panes unless there is a justified minimum/ideal range.
Prefer split, grid, and adaptive stacked arrangements over absolute positioning.

HIGH DPI / RETINA (HiDPI) SUPPORT
For macOS high-resolution displays:
- Point-Based Layout: Design layouts using points (not pixels). Let SwiftUI scale to physical pixels automatically.
- Layout Scaling: Use `@ScaledMetric` property wrapper for layout values (padding, spacing) so spacing adapts with text/display scaling preferences.
- Pixel Drawing: For custom rendering or canvas rendering, use `NSView.convertRectToBacking(_:)` or `convertPointToBacking(_:)` to find exact physical pixel coordinates. Never hardcode scale factors.
- Vector Assets: Import vector graphics (PDF/SVG) instead of raster graphics (PNG/JPG) to ensure crisp scaling on high-density screens.

ANTI-PATTERNS
Fixed-Size Everywhere
Repeated hard-coded frames that break text growth and resizing.
Offset Layout
Using .offset or .position to “solve” normal alignment.
Padding Soup
Layering unrelated padding calls to fake spacing architecture.
GeometryReader Abuse
Measuring everything manually instead of using SwiftUI layout tools.
One Layout for All Conditions
Forcing the same horizontal arrangement at every width and text size.
Decorative Containers
Introducing extra stacks only to visually push views around.
Premature Custom Layout
Writing a Layout type for a problem already solved by stacks, grids, frames, or ViewThatFits.
CHECKLIST
Before finalizing layout, verify:
Did I start with a built-in container?
Is the chosen container semantically correct?
Are text and controls allowed to size naturally?
Are spacing and alignment explicit and systematic?
Am I using padding, frame, and Spacer for their real purposes?
Does the layout adapt to narrow widths and larger text?
Am I avoiding offset/position hacks?
Is custom layout truly necessary?
Will this layout still work in a resized macOS window?
Could another developer understand the layout strategy in one pass?
ENFORCEMENT SUMMARY
Always prefer:
built-in layout containers first
natural content sizing
text-aware adaptation
explicit alignment and spacing
adaptive layout switching
custom layout only when justified
Never allow:
frame-heavy rigid layouts
offset-based layout architecture
arbitrary spacing accumulation
default GeometryReader usage for ordinary layout
premature custom layout types
